This is the README file for GPU version of GMP-ECM.
The GPU code will only work with NVIDIA GPU of compute capability greater
than 3.5.

Table of contents of this file

1. How to enable GPU code in GMP-ECM
2. Basic Usage
3. Advanced Usage
4. Known issues

##############################################################################

1. How to enable GPU code in GMP-ECM

By default the GPU code is not enabled, to enable it you have to follow the 
instructions of INSTALL-ecm until the 'configure' step. Then add "--enable-gpu".
(Earlier versions needed the CGBN library and "--with-cgbn-include=..."; it is
no longer used, and that option is accepted but ignored.)

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu
	
This will configure the code for NVIDIA GPU for all compute capabilities
between 3.5 and 9.0 known to the nvcc compiler.

To enable only a single compute capability you can set '--enable-gpu=XX'

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu=61 [other options]

By default, GMP-ECM will look for cuda.h in the default header directories,
but you can specify another directory, such as /opt/cuda, with:

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu --with-cuda=/opt/cuda

By default, GMP-ECM will look for the nvcc compiler in $PATH, but you can
specify another directory:

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu --with-cuda-bin=/PATH/DIR

For finer control you can specify the location of cuda.h as follows:

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu --with-cuda-include=/PATH/DIR

By default, GMP-ECM will look for CUDA the default library directories, but you
can specify another directory:

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu --with-cuda-lib=/PATH/DIR

Some versions of CUDA are not compatible with recent versions of gcc.
To specify which C compiler is called by the CUDA compiler nvcc, type:

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu --with-cuda-compiler=/PATH/DIR

The value of this parameter is directly passed to nvcc via the option
"--compiler-bindir". By default, GMP-ECM lets nvcc choose what C compiler it
uses.

If you get errors about "cuda.h: present but cannot be compiled"
Try setting CC to a know good gcc, you may need to use and --with-cuda-compiler

  $ ./configure --enable-gpu CC=gcc-8

Then, to compile the code, type:

  $ make

And to check that the program works correctly, type:

  $ make check

Additional randomized checks can be run with

  $ sage check_gpuecm.sage ./ecm

For failing kernels some additional information may be present in cuda-memcheck

  $ echo "(2^997-1)" | cuda-memcheck ./ecm -gpu -gpucurves 4096 -v 16000 0

##############################################################################

2. Basic Usage

To use your GPU for step 1, just add the -gpu option:

  $ echo "(2^835+1)/33" | ./ecm -gpu 1e4

It will compute both step 1 and step 2 on the GPU.

Step 2 on the GPU is a baby-step giant-step prime continuation, for inputs
of every size step 1 takes: it covers exactly the primes in (B2min, B2], at a
cost linear in B2.  Its default B2 is
therefore 100 * B1, much smaller than the CPU default (which is tuned for the
CPU's FFT continuation, whose cost grows much more slowly with B2).  A B2 given
on the command line is always used as is.

To do step 2 on the CPU instead (one curve at a time, with the FFT
continuation and the usual default B2), add "-cpustage2":

  $ echo "(2^835+1)/33" | ./ecm -gpu -cpustage2 1e4

"-gpupoly" (instead of "-gpu") runs that FFT continuation on the GPU: the
same polynomials and the same numbers as GMP-ECM's stage 2 on the CPU
(the same "Product of G(f_i)" at -v -v -v, the same factors), with its
default B2 and Brent-Suyama polynomial, curve by curve.  It always uses the
parameters of GMP-ECM's NTT stage 2 (dF a power of 2), lowered if needed to
fit the GPU memory; to compare with the CPU above 1920 bits, give the CPU
run "-ntt".  It takes inputs up to 98300 bits; above that, or without enough
GPU memory, step 2 runs on the CPU.  It is not yet tuned (curves are not
batched), and not yet the default.

  $ echo "(2^835+1)/33" | ./ecm -gpupoly 1e6

The only parametrization compatible with GPU code is "-param 3".

You can save the end of step 1 with "-save" and resume it later, on the GPU
too:

  $ ./ecm -gpu -resume saved.txt 1e4 1e6

With "-gpu", consecutive lines for the same number that are ECM with
"-param 3" and the same B1done are resumed together as one GPU batch (their
sigmas need not be consecutive; "-gpucurves n" caps the batch size).  If B1 is
larger than the saved B1done, step 1 is continued on the GPU from B1done to
B1 (giving exactly the residue a run from scratch to B1 would), then step 2
runs as usual.  A line the GPU cannot take (another method or parametrization)
is resumed on the CPU.  Reading the save file from a pipe ("-resume -")
works, but gives batches of one curve.

The options "-mpzmod", "-modmuln", "-redc", "-nobase2" and "-base2" have no
effect on the GPU steps; with "-cpustage2" they apply to step 2.

##############################################################################

3. Advanced Usage

The option "-gpudevice n" forces the GPU code to be executed on device n. Nvidia
tool "nvidia-smi" can be used to know to which number is associated a GPU.
Moreover, you can use GMP-ECM option "-v" (verbose) to see the properties of the
GPU on which the code is run.

The option "-gpucurves n" forces GMP-ECM to compute n curves in parallel on the
GPU. By default, the number of curves is choose to fill completly the GPU. The
number of curves must be a multiple of the number of curves by multiprocessors
(which depend on the GPU) or else it would be rounded to the next multiple.

Inputs of any size up to 262140 bits run without recompiling.  Up to 98300
bits, numbers are held as n 32-bit limbs spread over TPI threads per curve
(L limbs each, n = TPI * L); kernels are compiled for every L, and TPI is
chosen at run time: 1 to 32 (the threads of one warp) for inputs up to 24572
bits, 64 or 128 (several warps of a block, trading limbs through shared
memory) above.  For each input size GMP-ECM times the possible (TPI, L)
shapes on the GPU and uses the fastest ("-v" prints the timings and the
choice).  The input must be below 2^(32n - 4).

By default the number of curves is the number that fills the GPU for the
chosen shape.  Throughput for different "-gpucurves" values can be tested
using the provided "gpu_throughput_test.sh" script.  This takes optional ecm
command and number of curves.

    $ ./gpu_throughput_test.sh [ECM_CMD] [GPUCURVES]

Above 24572 bits there is also a step 1 kernel that multiplies with
number-theoretic transforms (NTT) over three 30-bit primes; it is timed
against the 64- and 128-thread kernels and used when faster, and it is the
only one above 98300 bits.  Each curve gets one thread block, and each number
is held as W = L/2 limbs for a transform length L from 2^10 to 2^14 (the
input must be below 2^(32W - 4)).  Step 2 runs on the GPU with whichever
of these kernels step 1 used.

The environment variable GMPECM_GPU_KERNEL=TPI or TPI:L forces a shape
(for testing); GMPECM_GPU_KERNEL=ntt forces the NTT kernel for any size, and
GMPECM_NTT_THREADS=n sets its threads per block (a power of 2, at most L/8).

##############################################################################

4. Known issues

If you get "Error msg: forward compatibility was attempted on non supported HW"
or "error: 'cuda.h' and 'cudart' library have different versions", then you
can look at https://stackoverflow.com/questions/43022843/nvidia-nvml-driver-library-version-mismatch/45319156#45319156.
In general the best solution is to restart the machine.

##############################################################################

Please report to sethtroisi (by email or on mersenneforum) any problems,
bugs, or observations.

